=== Collection ===
+ Ordered -> The order is of the insertion, or have a previsible order of iteration
+ Sorted -> The order is based on a standard (imposed by a Comparator, using the method compareTo())

= Big-O Notation =
Always consider this before choose a Collection
+ Time complexity
+ Space complexity

== Comparison ==
To compare collections, you need to implement one of these classes

= Comparable =
When you want to use Collections.sort(object<class>), the class needs to implement compareTo from Comparable
+ returns negative if lower than
+ returns 0 if equals to
+ returns positive if greater than

= Comparator =
It's useful as Comparable, but is generally used when you wants to compare objects without modify the logic of comparison

== Binary Search ==
A way to search in a Collection
+ Needs to be sorted
+ returns the index or (-(insertion point) -1)

== List ==
Can initialize with:
Arrays.asList(o1, o2, o3...) -> May change the values, but don't allow changes in the structure (add or remove)
List.of(o1, o2, o3...) -> Immutable lists

== Iterator ==
A class which checks before do an action (hasNext(), next())
Don't remove objects from a collection inside a foreach, this throws ConcurrentModificationException
+ Safe way to remove in foreach
+ listObject.iterator()

== Set ==
Unique elements (based on equals of the class)
+ HashSet -> Ordered by hash
+ LinkedHashSet -> Ordered by insertion position

= SortedSet =
Don't consider equals, only compareTo to find unique elements (this may cause problems if is different)
+ NavigableSet -> An interface with some additional methods to get elements based on already existing positions
+ TreeSet -> To order the class needs to have comparable, or you can add a comparator on the constructor

== Queue ==
Needs a class Comparable (like the SortedSet) to order by a determined priority
Have a good performance to remove and insert elements
+ FIFO (First In, First Out)

=== Map ===
+ Key & Value
Isn't a collection
Doesn't accept duplicate keys (KeySet)
Can iterate over with key and value like: "Entry.Map(Key, Value) referenceName : map.entrySet()"
+ HashMap -> Ordered by hash
+ LinkedHashMap -> Ordered by insertion position

== SortedMap ==
Don't consider equals, only compareTo to find unique elements (this may cause problems if is different)
+ NavigableMap -> An interface with some additional methods to get elements based on already existing positions
+ TreeMap -> Order by comparing the keys (compareTo)